<BaseLayout title="Acupressure for Menstrual Cramps: Best Points" description="Use acupressure for menstrual cramps and period pain. Guide to SP6, CV4, CV6, LI4, and other points that ease pelvic pain and cramping." breadcrumbs={[{ "label": "Home", "href": "/" }, { "label": "Conditions", "href": "/conditions" }, { "label": "Acupressure for Menstrual Cramps", "href": "/conditions/acupressure-for-menstrual-cramps" }]} datePublished="2026-08-27" dateModified="2026-08-27" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6"><h1 class="mb-4">Acupressure for Menstrual Cramps: Best Points for Period Pain Relief</h1><p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Period pain (dysmenorrhea) is one of the most common reasons people try acupressure. Several points sit along the lower abdomen and lower leg and are traditionally used to ease cramping and pelvic discomfort. The routine below is a practical, drug-free option you can use at home alongside heat, rest, and any care your clinician recommends.</p><div class="mb-8 p-4 bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 rounded-r-lg"><p class="text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><strong>Research Note:</strong> A number of small randomized trials have studied acupressure and acupuncture for menstrual pain, generally reporting lower pain scores than no treatment or usual care. The evidence is promising but limited and inconsistent in quality, so treat acupressure as supportive relief rather than a replacement for medical care.</p></div><section id="sp6" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/sp6-sanyinjiao-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">1. SP6 (Sanyinjiao) - Three Yin Intersection</a></h2><p class="mb-3">SP6 is the single most-used point for menstrual cramps in traditional Chinese medicine. It sits where the Spleen, Liver, and Kidney meridians converge on the lower leg, and is traditionally used to regulate the lower abdomen and ease pelvic pain.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the inside of the lower leg, about four finger-widths above the inner ankle bone, just behind the shinbone. <a href="/points/sp6-sanyinjiao-acupressure-point">SP6 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly with your thumb for 2-3 minutes on each leg. A deep, achy sensation is normal. Avoid this point during pregnancy.</p></section><section id="cv4" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/cv4-guanyuan-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">2. CV4 (Guanyuan) - Gate of Origin</a></h2><p class="mb-3">CV4 is a foundational lower-abdomen point used to warm and nourish the lower pelvis, where much of menstrual cramping is felt.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the midline of the lower abdomen, about three finger-widths below the navel. <a href="/points/cv4-guanyuan-acupressure-point">CV4 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Lie down, warm your hands, and press gently in a slow clockwise circle for 2-3 minutes. Combine with a heating pad for extra relief.</p></section><section id="cv6" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/cv6-qihai-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">3. CV6 (Qihai) - Sea of Qi</a></h2><p class="mb-3">CV6 sits just above CV4 and is traditionally used to support digestive and pelvic warmth, which many people find soothing during cramps.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> On the midline of the lower abdomen, about two finger-widths below the navel. <a href="/points/cv6-qihai-acupressure-point">CV6 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press gently with the flat of your fingers for 2-3 minutes, breathing slowly. Avoid deep pressure right over a full bladder.</p></section><section id="li4" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">4. LI4 (Hegu) - Joining Valley</a></h2><p class="mb-3">LI4 is one of the most versatile pain points in the body and is commonly paired with lower-abdomen points for cramping and discomfort.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> In the webbing between the thumb and index finger. <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point">LI4 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Squeeze the webbing firmly with the opposite thumb for 1-2 minutes per hand. Avoid during pregnancy.</p></section><section id="st36" class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4"><a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">5. ST36 (Zusanli) - Leg Three Miles</a></h2><p class="mb-3">ST36 supports overall digestive and lower-body circulation, and is often included in menstrual routines to settle the system alongside local points.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Location:</strong> About four finger-widths below the kneecap, one finger-width outward from the shinbone. <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point">ST36 guide</a>.</p><p class="mb-3"><strong>Technique:</strong> Press firmly with your thumb for 2-3 minutes on each leg.</p></section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">A Simple Cramps Routine</h2><p class="mb-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Start a few days before your period if you can. Spend 2-3 minutes on each leg at SP6 and ST36, then 2-3 minutes circling CV4 and CV6 on the lower abdomen, and finish with LI4 on both hands. A warm bath or heating pad afterward helps the muscles stay relaxed. Daily practice for a week tends to feel more useful than a single session.</p></section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="space-y-4"><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How quickly does it work?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For many people, gentle pressure on SP6 and the lower-abdomen points eases the worst of the cramping within 10-20 minutes, though relief is often temporary and the routine can be repeated.</p></details><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I use this while taking pain medication?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Yes. Acupressure is complementary and can be used alongside over-the-counter pain relief, heat, and any treatment your clinician recommends.</p></details><details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor?</summary><p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">See a clinician for severe pain that stops you from daily activity, pain that began suddenly or changed in pattern, pain with fever, very heavy bleeding, or if cramps do not improve with usual care. These can signal conditions such as endometriosis or pelvic inflammatory disease that need medical treatment.</p></details></div></section><hr class="my-8"><div class="bg-stone-100 dark:bg-stone-800 rounded-lg p-6 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400"><p><strong>Medical Disclaimer:</strong> This content is for informational purposes only and is not medical advice. Consult a licensed healthcare provider before starting any new wellness practice, especially if your periods are very painful or irregular.</p></div></article>
